# Configured 3×5 command deck from `kobo deck set` / `kobo deck push --sim`.
#
# The bar carries the page's own name rather than the word Deck, because a
# reader with a Build page and a Home page had to read the keys to tell which
# one they were looking at.
wait-for Todo
expect Home
expect Todo
expect Example
# A pushed preview says it is a preview. It used to say "Running on this
# device", which was a static layout claiming to be a live pairing.
expect Preview only. Pair to run commands.
expect-missing Pair with your computer
expect-missing Opening
expect-missing Refresh
clean
shot grid

# Pairing from the preview. The pairing screen names the one command that
# pairs the reader for you; it used to list three, including a trust step
# the companion now takes itself.
tap-id pair-preview
wait-for Pair with your computer
expect The first pairs this reader for you.
expect kobo sidekick init
expect kobo sidekick run
expect-missing kobo trust set sidekick
clean
shot pairing
tap-id enter-address
type 192.168.1.20
tap-id kb.enter
wait-for Now the pairing code
expect The six characters kobo sidekick init printed beside the address.
clean
shot code
# A code that could not be one is refused on its own step, not saved and
# later reported as a computer that does not answer.
tap-id enter-code
type zz
tap-id kb.enter
wait-for A pairing code is six letters and digits.
clean
shot code-error
# With no network the deck says so, rather than blaming the helper.
scenario offline
tap-id enter-code
type abc234
tap-id kb.enter
wait-for This reader is not on Wi-Fi.
expect-missing Check that Sidekick is open
clean
shot offline
# Pairing in the top bar is the way out of a pairing gone wrong, and the
# address it offers has the helper's port filled in.
tap-id repair
wait-for Pair with your computer
tap-id enter-address
expect 192.168.1.20:9331
# Back on the reader's keyboard puts it away; the second leaves pairing.
tap Back
tap back
wait-for This reader is not on Wi-Fi.
clean
shot back-to-deck
